add('III-hull-prerequisite','III',[127],'Remark 3.9.5(i), opening hypothesis and final well-definedness sentence',r'Suppose that we are in the situation of Proposition 3.9, (i). […] be a subset that contains a relatively compact subset whose log-volume [cf. the discussion of Remark 3.1.1, (iii), (iv), as well as Remark 3.9.7, (ii), below] is finite [i.e., $>-\infty$]. […] One verifies immediately that the holomorphic hull is well-defined [under the conditions stated].','Three excerpts in order from one item. […] omits the decorated ambient-space display, the definition, and its two branches. The surrounding source-context text states those branches.')
add('III-hull-properties','III',[127],'Remark 3.9.5(ii), displayed properties P1–P3',r'(P1) $\phi(H)=H$, for any $H\in\mathbb H$;\n\n(P2) $P\subseteq\phi(P)$, for any $P\in\mathbb P$;\n\n(P3) $\phi(P_1)\subseteq\phi(P_2)$, for any $P_1,P_2\in\mathbb P$ such that $P_1\subseteq P_2$.'.replace('\\n','\n'),'Complete list P1–P3. The preceding paragraph defines P as direct product regions, H as hulls, and φ as the hull map.')
add('III-weights','III',[94],'Remark 3.1.1(ii), normalized direct-sum weight display',r'When, moreover, we consider direct sums over all $\underline v\in\underline{\mathbb V}$ lying over a given $v_{\mathbb Q}\in\mathbb V_{\mathbb Q}$ as in the case of $\underline{\log}({}^{\alpha}\mathcal F_{v_{\mathbb Q}})$, it will be convenient to use the normalized weight\n\n$$\frac{1}{[K_{\underline v}:(F_{\mathrm{mod}})_v]\cdot\left(\sum_{\mathbb V_{\mathrm{mod}}\ni w\mid v_{\mathbb Q}}[(F_{\mathrm{mod}})_w:\mathbb Q_{v_{\mathbb Q}}]\right)}$$'.replace('\\n','\n'),'Complete lead-in sentence and its displayed weight. The following normalization explanation is omitted.',[dict(paper_symbol='underlined v; undecorated v',katex=r'\underline v;\ v',meaning='The chosen place of K and the corresponding place of F_mod. They are different symbols.')])
add('III-weight-warning','III',[95],'Remark 3.1.1(iii), displayed warning',r'such weighted sums of log-volumes do not, in general, arise as some positive real multiple of the [natural] logarithm of a “volume” or “measure” in the usual sense of measure theory.','Complete displayed warning; the preceding sentence supplies the weighted sums from item (ii).')
add('III-nonarch-normalization','III',[115],'Proposition 3.9(i), nonarchimedean packet-normalization sentence',r'Here, we assume that these log-volumes are normalized so that multiplication of an element of “$\mathfrak M(-)$” by $p_{\underline v}$ corresponds to adding the quantity $-\log(p_{\underline v})\in\mathbb R$; we shall refer to this normalization as the packet-normalization.','Complete sentence. Earlier on the page, M(-) is the set of nonempty compact open subsets and the local holomorphic integral structures have log-volume zero.')
add('III-procession-average','III',[116],'Proposition 3.9(i), final averaging sentence',r'Finally, when working with collections of capsules in a procession, as in Proposition 3.4, (ii), we obtain, in both the nonarchimedean and archimedean cases, log-volumes on the products of the “$\mathfrak M(-)$” associated to the various capsules under consideration, which we normalize by taking the average, over the various capsules under consideration; we shall refer to this normalization as the procession-normalization [cf. Remark 3.9.3 below].','Complete sentence. Earlier on this page the archimedean domain is compact closures of nonempty open subsets; the integral structures have zero log-volume.')
add('III-global-finite','III',[117],'Proposition 3.9(iii), finite local-volume support and summation passage',r'for the subset of elements whose components, indexed by $v_{\mathbb Q}\in\mathbb V_{\mathbb Q}$, have zero log-volume [cf. (i)] for all but finitely many $v_{\mathbb Q}\in\mathbb V_{\mathbb Q}$. Then, by adding the log-volumes of (i) [all but finitely many of which are zero!] at the various $v_{\mathbb Q}\in\mathbb V_{\mathbb Q}$, one obtains a global log-volume […]','Contiguous prose excerpt between the domain display and global log-volume display. Later invariance and degree assertions are outside this excerpt.')
